From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Respect the receiver's limit on slices per picture

Patch 0018 had x264 encode four slices per picture. A receiver states how
many slices it takes, and a min-slice-size of 0, which many televisions
send, means it does not take several. Four slices worked on the receivers
tried, but went beyond what the others promised.

x264 now encodes as many slices as the receiver takes, up to four, each in
its own thread, and a single slice in one thread for a receiver that takes
only one. A single slice of 1080p still encodes in about 5 ms, well inside
the pipeline latency.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -700,11 +700,12 @@
                     "tune", 0x4, /* zero latency */
                     "speed-preset", 1, /* ultrafast */
                     "rc-lookahead", 1,
-                    /* Four slices, each encoded by its own thread. Sliced
-                     * threading adds no frame of delay, unlike frame
-                     * threading, and cuts the time a frame takes to encode,
-                     * so bursts of motion no longer make it late. */
-                    "threads", 4,
+                    /* Up to four slices, each encoded by its own thread.
+                     * Sliced threading adds no frame of delay, unlike frame
+                     * threading, and cuts the time a frame takes to encode.
+                     * A receiver that does not take several slices per
+                     * picture gets one. */
+                    "threads", (guint) CLAMP (codec->max_slice_num, 1, 4),
                     "vbv-buf-capacity", 50,
                     "dct8x8", FALSE,
                     "ref", 1,
@@ -717,7 +718,7 @@
                     "interlaced", resolution->interlaced,
                     "bitrate",  bitrate_kbit,
                     "insert-vui", TRUE,
-                    "sliced-threads", TRUE,
+                    "sliced-threads", codec->max_slice_num > 1,
                     NULL);
       break;
 
